{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassTheory.QBetaC3ContinuumGeometry","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassTheory.QBetaC3ContinuumGeometry","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Bounds","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral.FundThmCalculus","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Integrability.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Integrability.LogMeromorphic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.vol_4_1_is_twice_tetrahedron_at_saddle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.vol_4_1_is_twice_tetrahedron_at_saddle","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L193-L209","doc":"**The retained continuum geometry.**  Only the geometry is kept; the\nearlier physical identification \"$c_3$ limits to $-\\mathrm{Vol}(4_1)$\" is\n**withdrawn as over-scoped** (anchor-blind — see the ⚠ CORRECTION note\nabove; and $c_3 = -2 = J_{\\mathrm{Hopf}}(1)$ is the canonical $q=1$\nshadow, not $-\\mathrm{Vol}(4_1)$).  What *is* true, and is recorded here\n(replacing the former vacuous `: True` placeholder that carried the false\n$c_3$ claim only in its name), is the geometric decomposition:\n\n- the figure-8 volume `Vol(4₁)` is exactly twice the regular-ideal-\n  tetrahedron volume `Λ(π/3)` (built from two ideal tetrahedra), and\n- that tetrahedron volume sits at the `π/3` cyclotomic saddle point of\n  the continuum action (`deriv classical_potential (π/3) = 0`,\n  `saddle_point_root`). ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:rem:q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry","label":"rem:q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.vol_4_1","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.vol_4_1","kind":"def","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L188-L191","doc":"The hyperbolic volume of the $4_1$ (figure-eight) knot manifold, built \nfrom exactly two regular ideal tetrahedra. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.vol_regular_ideal_tetrahedron","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.vol_regular_ideal_tetrahedron","kind":"def","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L183-L186","doc":"The geometric volume of a regular ideal tetrahedron evaluates identically \nto the critical saddle limit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.saddle_point_root","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.saddle_point_root","kind":"theorem","line":166,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L166-L181","doc":"The analytical saddle point bounding the non-compact contour integration \nis rigorously pinned to the $\\pi/3$ root. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.potential_derivative","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.potential_derivative","kind":"theorem","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L147-L164","doc":"The fundamental derivative of the classical topological potential \nforces the continuous saddle-point geometry. \n(Verified by reducing to Mathlib's interval integral FTC). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.f_continuousAt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.f_continuousAt","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L128-L145","doc":"Lemma 3: The integrand is continuous strictly inside the topological domain \n(avoiding the roots of unity at 0 and 2π). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.f_measurable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.f_measurable","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L121-L126","doc":"Lemma 2: The integrand is strongly measurable around the evaluation point. \n(Derived strictly from its global measurability). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.f_measurable_global","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.f_measurable_global","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L117-L119","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.f_integrable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.f_integrable","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L97-L115","doc":"Lemma 1: The singularity of the Lobachevsky integrand at t=0 is integrable.\nThe integrand `log|2 sin(t/2)|` decomposes as `log 2 + log|sin(t/2)|`.\nMathlib's `intervalIntegrable_log_sin` handles `log ∘ sin` for any\nendpoints; the constant `log 2` is trivially integrable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.log_integrable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.log_integrable","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L90-L95","doc":"Lemma 1.2: The natural logarithm is improperly integrable at 0.\nProved using Mathlib's `intervalIntegrable_log'` which handles the\nsingularity at 0 via dominated convergence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.f_integrable_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.f_integrable_bound","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L71-L88","doc":"Lemma 1.1: The singularity of the Lobachevsky integrand at t=0 is bounded by the natural logarithm. \nThis is the core asymptotic bound required for the improper Lebesgue integral. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.classical_potential","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.ContinuumGeometry.classical_potential","kind":"def","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/q-beta-c3-continuum-geometry.lean#L65-L69","doc":"The Lobachevsky function (the imaginary part of the quantum dilogarithm), \nrepresenting the continuous classical potential of the Faddeev integral.\nDefined formally via Mathlib interval integrals. ","signatureChunk":0}]}